WEBVTT

NOTE whisper small.en

00:00:00.430 --> 00:00:06.310
Machine learning and AI algorithms are increasingly trusted to help with or to make extremely

00:00:06.310 --> 00:00:07.310
important decisions.

00:00:07.310 --> 00:00:12.510
And so we'd like to make sure that as much as possible, that they're free of undesirable

00:00:12.510 --> 00:00:16.630
forms of bias, such as gender bias, ethnicity bias, and so on.

00:00:16.630 --> 00:00:21.590
What I want to do in this video is show you some of the ideas for diminishing or eliminating

00:00:21.590 --> 00:00:24.470
these forms of bias in word embeddings.

00:00:24.470 --> 00:00:29.070
When I use the term bias in this video, I don't mean the bias variance sense, the

00:00:29.070 --> 00:00:33.950
bias, instead I mean gender, ethnicity, sexual orientation bias.

00:00:33.950 --> 00:00:38.830
There's a different sense of bias than is typically used in the technical discussion

00:00:38.830 --> 00:00:40.390
on machine learning.

00:00:40.390 --> 00:00:45.470
But to motivate the problem, we talked about how word embeddings can learn analogies like

00:00:45.470 --> 00:00:47.750
man is the woman, the skin is the queen.

00:00:47.750 --> 00:00:52.950
But what if you ask it, man is the computer programmer as woman is the what?

00:00:52.950 --> 00:01:00.750
And so the authors of this paper, Toga Bulabasi, Kai Weichang, James Zhou, Van Kitech, Sally

00:01:00.750 --> 00:01:05.990
Grammer, and Adam Kallai found this somewhat horrifying result where a learned word embedding

00:01:05.990 --> 00:01:11.310
might output man is the computer programmer as woman is to homemaker.

00:01:11.310 --> 00:01:17.590
And that just seems wrong and it enforces a very unhealthy gender stereotype.

00:01:17.590 --> 00:01:21.110
And be much more preferable to have the algorithm output, man is the computer programmer

00:01:21.110 --> 00:01:23.230
as woman is the computer programmer.

00:01:23.230 --> 00:01:27.830
And they found also father is the doctor as mother is the what.

00:01:27.830 --> 00:01:32.990
And the really unfortunate result is that some learned word embeddings will output mother

00:01:32.990 --> 00:01:34.650
is the nurse.

00:01:34.650 --> 00:01:39.670
So word embeddings can reflect the gender, ethnicity, age, sexual orientation, and other

00:01:39.670 --> 00:01:43.110
biases of the text used to train the model.

00:01:43.110 --> 00:01:48.630
One that I'm especially passionate about is bias relating to social economic status.

00:01:48.630 --> 00:01:53.150
I think that every person, whether you come from a wealthy family or a low income family

00:01:53.150 --> 00:01:57.950
or anywhere in between, I think everyone should have great opportunities.

00:01:57.950 --> 00:02:03.670
And because machine learning algorithms are being used to make very important decisions,

00:02:03.670 --> 00:02:08.470
they're influencing everything ranging from college admissions to the way people find

00:02:08.470 --> 00:02:14.630
jobs to loan applications, whether your application for a loan gets approved to in the criminal

00:02:14.630 --> 00:02:18.550
justice system, even sentencing guidelines.

00:02:18.550 --> 00:02:21.710
Learning algorithms are making very important decisions.

00:02:21.710 --> 00:02:27.630
And so I think it's important that we try to change learning algorithms to diminish as

00:02:27.630 --> 00:02:33.950
much as is possible, or ideally eliminate these types of undesirable biases.

00:02:33.950 --> 00:02:38.630
Now in the case of word embeddings, they can pick up the biases of the text used

00:02:38.630 --> 00:02:40.670
to train the model.

00:02:40.670 --> 00:02:47.310
And so the biases they pick up will tend to reflect the biases in text, as is written

00:02:47.310 --> 00:02:48.310
by people.

00:02:48.550 --> 00:02:53.750
Over many decades, over many centuries, I think humanity has made progress in reducing these

00:02:53.750 --> 00:02:55.150
types of bias.

00:02:55.150 --> 00:03:00.270
And I think maybe fortunately for AI, I think we actually have better ideas to quickly reducing

00:03:00.270 --> 00:03:05.710
the bias in AI than for quickly reducing the bias in the human race.

00:03:05.710 --> 00:03:09.430
Although I think we're by no means done for AI as well.

00:03:09.430 --> 00:03:14.750
And there's still a lot of research and hardware to be done to reduce these types of biases

00:03:14.750 --> 00:03:16.150
in our learning algorithm.

00:03:16.150 --> 00:03:21.350
But what I want to do in this video is share with you one example of a set of ideas due

00:03:21.350 --> 00:03:27.550
to the paper referenced at the bottom by Bola Basi and others on reducing the bias in word

00:03:27.550 --> 00:03:30.580
embeddings.

00:03:30.580 --> 00:03:32.340
So here's the idea.

00:03:32.340 --> 00:03:34.900
Let's say that we've already learned a word embedding.

00:03:34.900 --> 00:03:39.180
So the word babysitter is here.

00:03:39.180 --> 00:03:43.820
The word doctor is here.

00:03:43.820 --> 00:03:52.740
We have a grandmother here and grandfather here.

00:03:52.740 --> 00:03:54.580
Maybe the word girl is embedded there.

00:03:54.580 --> 00:03:56.980
The word boy is embedded there.

00:03:56.980 --> 00:03:59.900
And maybe she is embedded here.

00:03:59.900 --> 00:04:01.860
And he is embedded there.

00:04:01.860 --> 00:04:08.580
So the first thing we're going to do is identify the direction corresponding to a particular

00:04:08.580 --> 00:04:11.860
bias we want to reduce or eliminate.

00:04:11.860 --> 00:04:15.660
And for illustration, I'm going to focus on gender bias.

00:04:15.660 --> 00:04:20.020
But these ideas are applicable to all of the other types of bias that I mentioned on the

00:04:20.020 --> 00:04:22.100
previous slide as well.

00:04:22.100 --> 00:04:31.340
So in this example, and so how do you identify the direction corresponding to the bias?

00:04:31.340 --> 00:04:36.460
For the case of gender, what you can do is take the embedding vector for he and subtract

00:04:36.460 --> 00:04:44.540
the embedding vector for she because that differs by gender and take e, male, subtract

00:04:44.540 --> 00:04:48.780
e, female, and take a few of these and average them.

00:04:48.780 --> 00:04:52.020
And take a few of these differences and basically average them.

00:04:52.020 --> 00:04:57.660
And this will allow you to figure out in this case that what looks like this direction

00:04:57.660 --> 00:05:02.780
is the gender direction, the bias direction.

00:05:02.780 --> 00:05:10.700
Whereas this direction is unrelated to the particular bias we're trying to address.

00:05:10.700 --> 00:05:16.700
So this is the non-bias direction.

00:05:16.700 --> 00:05:21.780
And in this case, the bias direction, think of this as a 1D subspace, whereas the non-bias

00:05:21.780 --> 00:05:27.780
direction, this would be a 299 dimensional subspace.

00:05:27.780 --> 00:05:31.540
And I've simplified the description a little bit in the original paper.

00:05:31.540 --> 00:05:35.980
The bias direction can be higher than one dimensional and rather than taking an average

00:05:35.980 --> 00:05:39.980
as an ascribing here, it's actually found using a more complicated algorithm called

00:05:39.980 --> 00:05:45.900
the SVD, a singular value decomposition, which is closely related to if you're familiar

00:05:45.900 --> 00:05:50.460
with principal components analysis and use this idea similar to the PC or the principal

00:05:50.460 --> 00:05:53.900
components analysis algorithm.

00:05:53.900 --> 00:05:57.340
After that, the next step is a neutralization step.

00:05:57.340 --> 00:06:02.740
So for every word that's not definitional, project it to get rid of bias.

00:06:02.740 --> 00:06:07.460
So there are some words that intrinsically capture gender.

00:06:07.460 --> 00:06:10.220
So words like grandmother, grandfather, girlboy, she, he.

00:06:10.220 --> 00:06:13.820
You know, a gender is intrinsic in the definition.

00:06:13.820 --> 00:06:20.260
Whereas there are other words like doctor and babysitter that we want to be gender neutral.

00:06:20.260 --> 00:06:23.460
And really in the more general case, you might want words like doctor, babysitter to

00:06:23.460 --> 00:06:28.420
be ethnicity neutral or sexual orientation neutral and so on.

00:06:28.420 --> 00:06:33.260
But we'll just use gender as the illustrating example here.

00:06:33.260 --> 00:06:39.540
But so for every word that is not definitional, this basically means not words like grandmother

00:06:39.540 --> 00:06:45.420
and grandfather, which really have a very legitimate gender component because by definition,

00:06:45.420 --> 00:06:48.700
grandmothers are female and grandfathers are male.

00:06:48.700 --> 00:06:55.580
And so for words like doctor and babysitter, let's just project them onto this axis to

00:06:55.580 --> 00:07:01.260
reduce their components or to eliminate their components in the bias direction to reduce

00:07:01.260 --> 00:07:06.100
a component in this horizontal direction.

00:07:06.100 --> 00:07:08.580
So that's the second neutralized step.

00:07:08.580 --> 00:07:13.380
And then the final step is called equalization in which you might have pairs of words such

00:07:13.380 --> 00:07:24.660
as grandmother and grandfather or a girl and boy where you want the only difference

00:07:24.660 --> 00:07:27.900
in their embedding to be the gender.

00:07:27.900 --> 00:07:30.580
And so in this, and so why do you want that?

00:07:30.580 --> 00:07:37.060
Well in this example, the distance or the similarity between babysitter and grandmother

00:07:37.060 --> 00:07:42.780
is actually smaller than the distance between babysitter and grandfather.

00:07:42.780 --> 00:07:48.700
And so this maybe reinforces an unhealthy or maybe undesirable bias that grandmothers

00:07:48.700 --> 00:07:51.020
end up babysitting more than grandfathers.

00:07:51.020 --> 00:07:56.260
So in the final equalization step, what we would like to do is to make sure that words

00:07:56.260 --> 00:08:00.660
like grandmother and grandfather are both exactly the same similarity or exactly the

00:08:00.660 --> 00:08:05.580
same distance from words that should be gender neutral, such as babysitter or such

00:08:05.580 --> 00:08:06.780
as doctor.

00:08:06.780 --> 00:08:10.700
So there are a few linear algebra steps for that, but what they will basically do is

00:08:10.700 --> 00:08:19.100
move grandmother and grandfather to a pair of points that are equally distant from this

00:08:19.100 --> 00:08:21.060
axis in the middle.

00:08:21.060 --> 00:08:26.140
And so the effect of that is that now the distance between babysitter compared to these

00:08:26.140 --> 00:08:29.580
two words will be exactly the same.

00:08:29.580 --> 00:08:35.060
And so in general, there are many pairs of words like this, grandmother, grandfather,

00:08:35.060 --> 00:08:44.500
boy girl, sorority, fraternity, girlhood, boyhood, sister, brother, niece, nephew, daughter,

00:08:44.500 --> 00:08:49.820
son that you might want to carry out through this equalization step.

00:08:49.820 --> 00:08:54.700
So the final detail is how do you decide what word to neutralize?

00:08:54.700 --> 00:09:01.100
So for example, the word doctor seems like a word you should neutralize to make it non-gender

00:09:01.100 --> 00:09:04.540
specific or non-ethnicity specific.

00:09:04.540 --> 00:09:10.380
Whereas the words grandmother and grandmother should not be made non-gender specific.

00:09:10.380 --> 00:09:13.060
And there are also words like beard, right?

00:09:13.060 --> 00:09:17.380
That is just a statistical fact that men are much more likely to have beards than women.

00:09:17.380 --> 00:09:21.780
So maybe beards should be closer to male than female.

00:09:21.780 --> 00:09:31.460
And so what the authors did is train a classifier to try to figure out what words are definitional,

00:09:31.460 --> 00:09:34.700
what words should be gender specific and what words should not be.

00:09:34.700 --> 00:09:39.620
And it turns out that most words in the English language are not definitional, meaning that

00:09:39.620 --> 00:09:41.820
gender is not part of the definition.

00:09:41.820 --> 00:09:45.980
And this is a relatively small subset of words like this, grandmother, grandfather,

00:09:45.980 --> 00:09:53.420
girl, boy, sorority, fraternity, and so on that should not be neutralized.

00:09:53.420 --> 00:09:58.740
And so a linear classifier can tell you what words to pass through the neutralization

00:09:58.740 --> 00:10:06.020
step to project out this bias direction to project it onto this essentially 299 dimensional

00:10:06.020 --> 00:10:08.300
subspace.

00:10:08.300 --> 00:10:12.180
And then finally, the number of pairs you want to equalize, that's actually also relatively

00:10:12.180 --> 00:10:19.660
small and is at least for the gender example, it is quite feasible to handpick most of

00:10:19.660 --> 00:10:22.700
the pairs you want to equalize.

00:10:22.700 --> 00:10:27.140
So the full algorithm is a bit more complicated than I presented here.

00:10:27.140 --> 00:10:30.140
You can take a look at the paper for the full details.

00:10:30.140 --> 00:10:35.540
And you also get to play a few of these ideas in the program exercises as well.

00:10:35.540 --> 00:10:40.260
So to summarize, I think that reducing or eliminating bias of our learning algorithms

00:10:40.260 --> 00:10:45.100
is a very important problem, because these algorithms are being asked to help with or

00:10:45.100 --> 00:10:48.740
to make more and more important decisions in society.

00:10:48.740 --> 00:10:52.820
In this video, I shared just one set of ideas for how to go about trying to address

00:10:52.820 --> 00:10:53.820
this problem.

00:10:53.820 --> 00:11:00.260
But this is still very much an ongoing area of active research by many researchers.

00:11:00.260 --> 00:11:03.740
So that's it for this week's videos.

00:11:03.740 --> 00:11:07.740
Best of luck with this week's program exercises, and I look forward to seeing you next week.
